kosha arch

  -- the arch: pure compression geometry. the oldest long-span structural form.
  -- a semicircle (or other curve) of wedge-shaped blocks (voussoirs).
  -- gravity loads convert to compression along the arch thrust line.
  -- no tension anywhere — this is why arches work in masonry (stone, brick).
  -- stone has no tensile strength but very high compressive strength.
  --
  -- how it works:
  --   each voussoir (wedge block) is squeezed between its neighbours.
  --   the compression follows the arch curve — the thrust line.
  --   the thrust line must stay within the middle third of the arch material.
  --   at the base (spring point): horizontal thrust pushes outward.
  --   this thrust must be resisted by abutments (buttresses, side walls, tie rods).
  --   if the abutments spread: arch collapses. this is why arches need thick walls.
  --
  -- the catenary: the shape a hanging chain naturally takes under its own weight.
  --   if inverted → the shape an arch should take for pure compression under its own weight.
  --   Antonio Gaudí used hanging chain models (inverted) to design Sagrada Família vaults.
  --   this is exact structural intuition: form as minimum-energy state.
  --
  -- arch forms:
  --   semicircular:   Roman. strong. spans limited by span/rise = 2.
  --   pointed:        Gothic. allows steeper, taller arches. thrust more vertical.
  --   parabolic:      pure compression for uniform load. bridges.
  --   catenary:       pure compression for self-weight. domes, vaults.
  --   flat arch:      very high thrust. requires strong abutments.
  --
  -- the keystone: the topmost voussoir. locks the arch. last piece placed.
  -- without the keystone: the arch cannot stand. the keystone IS the closure.
  -- metaphorically: the keystone is the piece that makes the system complete.

